# Git Worktrees

For any task that writes or changes code, always work in a Cursor-managed worktree. Never edit the current checkout, including `master`, `main`, or another user's working branch. Read-only investigation may use the current checkout.

For any repo (including background or parallel agent work), rely on Cursor's native Worktrees feature for isolated checkouts. Native worktrees are created at launch time - via the Agents Window "run in worktree" option, the `/worktree` skill, or the CLI `-w` flag - and live under `[agent config path removed]`.

The location is the hard constraint. Do NOT create a worktree as a sibling directory (e.g. `[company path removed]`) or inside the repo. Worktrees in those places clutter the filesystem and can be picked up by MSBuild globs / Docker build context / IDE language servers.

If a code-change task is not already running inside a Cursor-managed worktree, create one before editing. You may run `git worktree add` yourself when necessary, as long as the new tree lives at `[agent config path removed]`. Call `move_agent_to_root` on that path immediately afterward, before making any changes.

Do not use the current checkout as a fallback when a worktree is unavailable. Pause and ask the user to relaunch in a worktree or approve creating one. Remove a self-created worktree with `git worktree remove` once the work is done.
